  "query_preview": "..\n\nNext 7 days (Through next Saturday)...\n\nShowers and some isolated embedded thunderstorms continue across \nour area, mainly to the east and south, in the early morning \nhours. Satellite imagery showed the upper trough axis extended \nfrom the Pac NW through northern WY at this time.  \n\nAs the trough slowly lifts north right across our CWA today we\nexpect to see areas of showers fill in due to synoptic ascent. \nInstability suggest some embedded thunderstorms as well, mainly \nacross our eastern zones through the day where a vorticity max \nwill track round the trough. Pockets of moderate to heavy rainfall\nare again possible, but to a lesser extent than Saturday thanks \nto deepening downslope flow and limited lapse rates compared to \nyesterday. Brisk downslope winds developing in the western CWA \n(20-35 mph gusts) will limit rainfall rates and coverage by \nafternoon.\n\nHigh elevation snow showers are likely as snow levels fall to \nnear 9kft today. A Winter Weather Advisory is in effect for \ntravel impacts over the Beartooth Highway, mainly above Vista \nPoint, through this evening. Look snowfall amounts of 2-4\" on W-NW\naspects.\n\nThe precipitation will finally all lift to the north this evening \nas synoptic ascent ends and drier air moves in behind the trough \naxis drifting north. Additional precipitation amounts through \ntoday should generally average around 0.20\" west to 0.75\" east, \nbut no doubt there will be localized heavier amounts with any \nstronger convection.\n\nTemps today will be cool with highs in the 50s west to upper 60s\neast.  \n\nAs the current upper trough drifts over the MT/Canadian border\nMonday, another shortwave tracks through Idaho and merges in the \ncyclonic flow. Models are indicating this will produce scattered \nafternoon & evening storms Monday. Due to somewhat better shear, \nsubstantial diurnal heating and easterly surface winds, cannot \nrule out a few marginally severe storms late Monday (though \nmodels not showing this yet).\n\nUpper low moves slowly and gradually fills Tuesday and Wednesday,\nkeeping us under a cyclonic flow. Still a lower chance of showers\n& t-storms these days, with best chance of storms in the east, and\na gradual warming trend. Next Pacific wave arrives Thursday. \nOverall, active weather persists Monday through Thursday as temps \nwarm back to the 70s. Higher heights and a shift back to SW flow \naloft should push temps back to the 80s by the end of the week. \nFriday and Saturday look mostly dry for now, but we could see \nsome convection work into the Beartooths/Crazies by the weekend\nas the next Pacific coast trough sets up. BT",
